"Equations and Theories in (https://ueaeprints.uea.ac.uk/97676/1/DT%20241002%20Final%20Thesis.pdf)\nciteturn9550search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; In 1970, Knuth [24] exhibited a set of defining relations K for the plactic monoidsof the form xzy = zxy and yxz = yzx for x < y < z, x, y, z ∈A and xyx = yxx\nChapter 3: The plactic monoid: background\n26\n3.2\nPresentations of the plactic monoid\nIn 1970, Knuth [24] exhibited a set of defining relations K for the plactic monoids\nof the form xzy = zxy and yxz = yzx for x < y < z, x, y, z ∈A and xyx = yxx\nand yyx = yxy for x < y, x, y ∈A. That is, we have\nK = {xzy = zxy, x ≤y < z} ∪{yxz = yzx, x < y ≤z}\nwith Pn = ⟨A|K⟩. For each finite rank, it follows that Pn will be finitely presented.\nNote that the Knuth relations are equivalent to running Schensted’s algorithm on\nall words of length 3.\nThe Knuth relations are homogeneous: for each (ℓ, r) ∈K, |ℓ| = |r|. This namely\nmeans that the plactic monoid is multi-homogeneous1, and the content of an\nelement in Pn is well defined and additive: for each u, v ∈Pn, c(uv) = c(u) + c(v).\nIt was shown by Cain, Gray, and Malheiro in [6] that the plactic monoid admits a\nfinite complete rewriting system, which we describe here.\nWe consider two columns α, β as words in A∗. We say that α and β are compatible,\nwritten α ⪰β, if αβ is the column reading of a tableau. Then each pair α, β\nwith α ⪰̸ β yields a rewrite rule. Consider the tableau associated to P(αβ). Since\nthe number of columns in P(αβ) is the length of the longest increasing sequence,\nand α, β are columns, it follows that P(αβ) will be a tableau with at most two\ncolumns. Therefore this tableau will have column reading γδ, for some columns\nγ, δ with γ ⪰δ, and potentially δ = ε.\nNow consider C = {cα | α ∈A∗, α is a column} to be a set of symbols corresponding\nto columns in A∗. Since A is finite and columns are strictly decreasing sequences,\nC is also finite. Then define R to be the set of all rewrite rules detailed above\nR = {cαcβ →cγcδ| α, β ∈A∗α ⪰̸ β}.\n1i.e. that each of its relations is homogeneous.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) About the rewriting systems produced by the Knuth-Bendix algorithm (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/222805299_About_the_rewriting_systems_produced_by_the_Knuth-Bendix_algorithm)\nciteturn9550search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 43.6 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # About the rewriting systems produced by the Knuth-Bendix algorithm ... (weighted) lexicographic order is used. ... We present two methods for proving confluence of left-linear term rewrite systems.\n\n# About the rewriting systems produced by the Knuth-Bendix algorithm\n\n  * January 1983\n  * Information Processing Letters 16(1):31-34\n\nDOI:10.1016/0020-0190(83)90009-1\n\nAuthors:\n\nYves Métivier\n\n  * Laboratoire Bordelais de Recherche en Informatique\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe show that in a free F-algebra endowed with a strict order we can associate with a complete rewriting system in the sense of Knuth-Bendix an equivalent proper (minimal) complete system. Furthermore, if the complete system is compatible with a strict order, then the equivalent proper complete system is unique.\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nContent uploaded by Yves Métivier\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area was uploaded by Yves Métivier on Oct 09, 2017\n\nInformation Processing Letters 16 (1983) 31-34\n\nNorth-Holland Publishing Company\n\n24 January 1983\n\nABOUT THE REWRITING SYSTEMS PRODUCED BY THE KNUTH-BENDIX\n\nCOMPLETION ALGORITHM\n\nYves METIVIER\n\nUniversitk de Bordeaux I, U. E. R. de Matht+natiques et Informatique, 33405 Talence, France\n\nCommunicated by L. Boasson\n\nReceived July 1982\n\nRevised September 1982\n\nWe show that in a free F-algebra endowed with a strict order we can associate with a complete rewriting system in the sense\n\nof Knuth-Bendix an equivalent proper (minimal) complete system. Further